@startuml lifecycle
title Frontend lifecycle - reset, scheduled wakeup, drain and takeover
autonumber
hide footbox
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam maxMessageSize 95

legend left
  [E] Existing gem5 call / signal
  [N] New proposed integration
  [A] Illustrative local algorithm
endlegend

actor "Simulation / drain manager" as Sim
participant "CPU / event queue" as CPU
participant BAC
participant Gem5BpuBridge as Bridge
participant IBPU
participant FTQ
participant Fetch
participant BlockFetchEngine as Engine
participant "Decode / Rename / IEW" as Pipe
participant Commit

== Startup and per-thread reset ==
Sim -> CPU: [E] startup()
CPU -> CPU: [E] BaseCPU::startup()
CPU -> BAC: [E] startupStage()
BAC -> BAC: [E] resetStage()
loop each configured tid
    BAC -> BAC: [E] clearStates(tid): bacPC = cpu->pcState(tid)
    BAC -> FTQ: [E] resetState(tid)
    BAC -> Bridge: [N] clear frontend state for tid
    Bridge -> IBPU: [N] reset(tid, startPC)
    IBPU -> IBPU: [A] Cancel transient predictions, histories and timers;
    note right of IBPU
      Define reset as a speculative-context reset.
      Initial table allocation is model construction policy;
      clearing trained tables requires a separate explicit policy.
      This avoids losing training on ordinary stage reset.
    end note
end
BAC -> BAC: [E] switchToActive()
CPU -> Fetch: [E] startupStage() -> resetStage()
Fetch -> Engine: [N] clear cursor, segments and request bookkeeping
CPU -> Pipe: [E] startupStage(): Decode, IEW, Rename (this order)
CPU -> Commit: [E] startupStage()
note over CPU,Fetch
  Sources: cpu.cc:CPU::startup / CPU::removeThread;
  bac.cc:BAC::startupStage / resetStage / clearStates;
  fetch.cc:Fetch::startupStage / resetStage / clearStates.
  CPU::removeThread also calls BAC::clearStates(tid), then Fetch::clearStates(tid):
  use the same new cleanup hooks after normal pipeline quiescence.
end note

== Tick ordering and a model deadline while BAC cannot publish ==
CPU -> CPU: [E] tickEvent -> CPU::tick()
CPU -> BAC: [E] tick()
BAC -> BAC: [E] checkSignalsAndUpdate(tid)
BAC -> Bridge: [N] service(now = cpu->curCycle(), credits)
Bridge -> IBPU: [N] tick(now, credits)
note over BAC,IBPU
  Service pending model work even when BAC is FTQFull / FTQLocked.
  Admission and new query issue obey their separate credits.
  Stopped / invalid contexts cannot issue, but timers and recovery must progress.
  Feedback and recovery precede new issue (see tick_path.puml).
end note
Bridge -> IBPU: [N] nextWakeup()
IBPU --> Bridge: [N] optional absolute deadline in CPU-domain Cycles
Bridge -> CPU: [N] arm / update frontendWakeEvent(deadline)
CPU -> CPU: [N] delta = max(0, deadline - curCycle());
CPU -> CPU: [N] fireTick = clockEdge(Cycles(delta)); schedule earliest wake
note right of CPU
  New CPU-owned event, not schedule(event, raw Cycles).
  Order its callback before CPU_Tick_Pri at the deadline edge.
  Re-arm on an earlier deadline and on clock-domain changes.
  Ensure at most one CPU tick per edge; an already serviced
  deadline must not recursively schedule the same cycle.
  nextWakeup excludes a ready response waiting only for credits;
  FTQ credit release / external callbacks wake blocked work.
end note
CPU -> Fetch: [E] tick()
CPU -> Pipe: [E] tick(): Decode -> Rename -> IEW
CPU -> Commit: [E] tick()
CPU -> CPU: [E] advance time buffers, activity accounting, tryDrain()
alt pipeline remains active
    CPU -> CPU: [E] schedule(tickEvent, clockEdge(Cycles(1)))
else activity becomes idle but a model deadline is pending
    CPU -> CPU: [N] retain frontendWakeEvent despite CPU tick idling
    ... simulation event time reaches fireTick ...
    CPU -> CPU: [N] frontendWakeEvent callback, if context is runnable
    CPU -> CPU: [N] wakeCPU accounting + ensure earliest CPU tick is scheduled
    CPU -> BAC: [E] resumed CPU::tick() starts with BAC::tick()
    BAC -> Bridge: [N] service(now, credits), process due work once
end
note over CPU,IBPU
  Sources: cpu.cc:CPU::tick / CPU::wakeCPU;
  cpu.hh:CPU::scheduleTickEvent; bac.cc:BAC::tick.
  Existing wakeCPU can return if activityRec.active(); the new scheduling helper
  must still guarantee a tick when no tickEvent is scheduled.
  Recompute / cancel the model wake on reset, recovery, drain and switchOut.
  Suspended threads do not resume architectural execution solely for a prediction.
end note

== Drain: let Commit reach a safe point before stopping its thread ==
Sim -> CPU: [E] drain()
CPU -> Commit: [E] drain(): drainPending = true
CPU -> CPU: [E] isCpuDrained(); otherwise wakeCPU() and record activity
note over CPU,IBPU
  Do not globally stop prediction here: Commit may need additional instructions
  to reach a safe architectural boundary. Already idle threads need no new issue.
  beginDrain() has no tid argument: invoke it once every participating thread
  has stopped; apply per-thread stopping / cleanup as each safe point arrives.
end note
loop normal CPU ticks until each participating thread reaches a safe point
    CPU -> BAC: [E] tick()
    BAC -> Bridge: [N] service(now, credits for still-running threads)
    CPU -> Fetch: [E] tick()
    CPU -> Pipe: [E] tick(): Decode -> Rename -> IEW
    CPU -> Commit: [E] tick() -> commitInsts()
    Commit -> Commit: [E] safe boundary: squashAfter(tid, head_inst)
    Commit -> CPU: [E] commitDrained(tid)
    CPU -> BAC: [E] drainStall(tid)
    BAC -> Bridge: [N] mark this thread stopping; reject new issue / admission
    CPU -> Fetch: [E] drainStall(tid)
end
note over BAC,Commit
  Sources: commit.cc:Commit::commitInsts (drainPending safe-point check);
  cpu.cc:CPU::commitDrained; bac.cc:BAC::drainStall;
  fetch.cc:Fetch::drainStall. Commit runs after BAC / Fetch in the same tick.
  Normal squash feedback is still processed before the BAC drain-stall branch.
end note
CPU -> BAC: [E] later tick(): checkSignalsAndUpdate(tid)
BAC -> Bridge: [N] retire prior commit feedback; discard uncommitted predictions for tid
Bridge -> IBPU: [N] onRecovery(drain cut, restartPC) as needed, exactly once
BAC -> FTQ: [E] squash(tid)
BAC -> BAC: [E] bacStatus[tid] = Idle
note over BAC,IBPU
  Existing drain branch calls squashBpuHistories(tid) then FTQ::squash(tid).
  With bpuModel selected, bridge / IBPU own the equivalent history cleanup;
  do not call legacy BPredUnit history functions on custom payloads.
end note
opt all threads safe / idle, drain not yet sent to model
    BAC -> Bridge: [N] initiate model drain
    Bridge -> IBPU: [N] beginDrain()
    IBPU -> IBPU: [A] stop issue; cancel speculative work / control events;
    IBPU -> IBPU: [A] release metadata after feedback users are finished
end
CPU -> Fetch: [E] tick(): consume squash / drain stall
Fetch -> Engine: [N] discard speculative segments; mark request tags canceled
note over Fetch,Engine
  Preserve existing ITLB / I-cache response and retry ownership rules.
  Discarding a byte buffer does not cancel an accepted memory request.
  Drain must also account for custom outstanding tags, callbacks and fault events;
  clear them on completion or explicit ownership transfer, never by forgetting them.
end note
loop until all retained callbacks / metadata have been resolved
    CPU -> CPU: [E] tryDrain() -> isCpuDrained()
    CPU -> BAC: [E] isDrained()
    BAC -> IBPU: [N] isDrained() via bridge
    IBPU --> BAC: [N] no responses, overrides, pipeline work or live histories
    BAC --> CPU: [N] existing FTQ empty / BAC idle AND model / bridge drained
    CPU -> Fetch: [E] isDrained()
    Fetch -> Engine: [N] check custom buffers / callbacks / outstanding ownership
    Fetch --> CPU: [N] existing fetch predicate AND custom assembly drained
    CPU -> Pipe: [E] isDrained() for each stage
    CPU -> Commit: [E] isDrained()
end
CPU -> CPU: [N] cancel remaining frontendWakeEvent
CPU -> CPU: [E] deschedule tickEvent; signalDrainDone()
note over CPU,Fetch
  Sources: cpu.cc:CPU::drain / tryDrain / isCpuDrained;
  bac.cc:BAC::isDrained / drainSanityCheck;
  fetch.cc:Fetch::isDrained / drainSanityCheck / doSquash.
  Existing Fetch::isDrained checks fetchQueue / status / finishTranslationEvent;
  it is not a check of the proposed BlockFetchEngine request map.
  Already-drained CPU::drain returns Drained directly rather than signalDrainDone().
end note

== Resume or CPU takeover ==
alt same CPU resumes after drain
    Sim -> CPU: [E] drainResume()
    CPU -> BAC: [E] drainResume(): clear drain stalls
    BAC -> Bridge: [N] resume custom frontend
    Bridge -> IBPU: [N] resume()
    note right of IBPU
      Resume at the restart PC established by final drain recovery.
      Re-enable issue; keep trained tables.
      Do not invoke reset twice through both BAC and Fetch.
    end note
    CPU -> Fetch: [E] drainResume()
    CPU -> Commit: [E] drainResume()
    CPU -> CPU: [E] activateThread() for active contexts; schedule tick at nextCycle()
else drained CPU is switched out and a new CPU takes over
    Sim -> CPU: [E] switchOut(); later newCPU.takeOverFrom(oldCPU)
    CPU -> CPU: [N] ensure old frontendWakeEvent is canceled
    CPU -> CPU: [E] BaseCPU::takeOverFrom(oldCPU)
    CPU -> BAC: [E] takeOverFrom() -> resetStage() -> clearStates(tid)
    BAC -> Bridge: [N] same reset hook as startup
    Bridge -> IBPU: [N] reset(tid, cpu->pcState(tid)) on destination model
    CPU -> Fetch: [E] takeOverFrom() -> resetStage()
    Fetch -> Engine: [N] clear destination assembly state
    CPU -> Pipe: [E] takeOverFrom() for each stage
    CPU -> Commit: [E] takeOverFrom()
    CPU -> CPU: [E] leave Idle; later activation / drainResume starts execution
    note over CPU,IBPU
      Sources: cpu.cc:CPU::switchOut / takeOverFrom / drainResume;
      bac.hh:BAC::takeOverFrom; fetch.cc:Fetch::takeOverFrom.
      Model selection is construction-time Python configuration.
      Neither takeover nor reset automatically transfers trained predictor state.
      Checkpoint serialization or cross-model state migration needs its own contract.
    end note
end
@enduml
